## What happened

.NET 11 enforces `PlatformNotSupportedException` for **DSA** on macOS. Apple's native crypto framework (Security.framework) has never implemented DSA, and .NET previously shipped a managed/OpenSSL-based shim that has now been removed. On Linux and Windows, platform-native DSA support still exists, so those targets continue to work.

## How to fix

Since you only need to **verify** legacy signatures (not generate new ones), you have two main options:

### Option 1 – Use BouncyCastle (recommended, fully managed)

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsa(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters publicKey)
{
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is pure managed code—no platform dependency.

### Option 2 – Conditional OpenSSL on macOS

Install OpenSSL via Homebrew (`brew install openssl`) and P/Invoke into it only on macOS, keeping the built-in `DSA` class on Windows/Linux. This adds a native dependency and deployment complexity.

---

**Bottom line:** Prefer BouncyCastle for a cross-platform managed solution. If you can eventually retire the DSA signatures, migrate callers to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, which are supported everywhere.
